  "grammar": [
    { "id": "g-yesverb", "title": "Saying yes: repeat the verb", "body": "Chinese has no single word for 'yes'. Answer with the verb from the question: 你吃鱼吗？ → 吃！ (yes) or 不吃 (no). 你会用筷子吗？ → 会！", "examples": ["s-nichiyuma", "s-chi"] },
    { "id": "g-anota", "title": "要不要…? questions", "body": "Say a verb, then 不, then the verb again for a friendly yes/no question: 要不要喝茶？ = want some tea? You don't need 吗 with this pattern.", "examples": ["s-yaobuyaohecha"] },
    { "id": "g-taile", "title": "太…了 = too …", "body": "Wrap an adjective in 太…了: 太辣了 = too spicy. It can also be a happy 'so…!': 太好了！ = great!", "examples": ["s-taila"] },
    { "id": "g-xiang", "title": "想 + verb = would like to", "body": "想 before a verb is a soft way to say what you'd like: 你想喝什么？ = what would you like to drink?", "examples": ["s-nixiangheshenme"] },
    { "id": "g-hosting", "title": "Table manners in words", "body": "Hosts keep offering: 多吃点！(eat more), 别客气！(don't be shy), 再来一碗吧！(another bowl). Praise the food — 真好吃！— and when you're done, thank them and say 我吃饱了 (I'm full). It's normal to be offered again; a smile and 吃饱了 is enough.", "examples": ["s-bieqikeqi", "s-xiexieayi-chibaole"] }
  ],
